out.push(SAMPLE(['Mrs Oueidat was asked at the November hearing why she omitted the covering line from the record, and she did not omit it: she filed it, the registry did not read it into the record, and the question put the act on her. Mr Shihabi said so you say so, and that is what she did the first time, years ago, and it was a mistake, because I did not omit it makes the next ten minutes about who omitted it, and the hearing was not about that and the judge had not asked. Mrs Khoury said a witness who names the mistake has changed the subject to the mistake, and the reframe answers both questions and names nothing. Dr Qabbani asked her to say it again, and she gave it: if the question is whether the line was filed, it was, on the fourth of July, and the file reference is in my statement; if it is whether it was read into the record, it was not, and I was not asked to read it, which is thirty-four words, two answers and no accusation. Mrs Tarabishi said the judge still learns that the registry did not read it, and the judge learns it from the facts and not from the witness\'s opinion of them: a fact a judge has worked out is a fact, and a fact a witness has asserted about another office is a dispute. Mr Tayara asked whether if at the front is not rude, and not if it is unstressed, because stress on if turns the clause into a challenge and the questioner then defends the question. Mrs Rizk asked what happens if you cannot tell which question they mean, and then the reframe is doing exactly what it is for: you answer both and the questioner takes whichever one they wanted. Mr Attar asked whether there is a case where you say it plainly, and where the premise is something the questioner could not have known and will act on, once and early, as a fact. Mr Zahreddine said his was a surveyor who said he had not inspected the bays, and the scope paragraph says he did not, so the premise is true and the question is fair: about a fifth of the questions that feel wrong are correct and uncomfortable. Mrs Ajouz said so you test it first, and in the four seconds you have, with one question to yourself: is the thing they have assumed on the page or not? And Mrs Oueidat said the thing she will take away is the first time: she said I did not omit it and she was right, and she spent ten minutes being right about something nobody had asked, and the registry\'s clerk, who had done nothing deliberate, was in the room.']));
out.push(A('⚠ **The last line is the one to carry out of the cycle: she spent ten minutes being right about something nobody had asked, and the registry\'s clerk, who had done nothing deliberate, was in the room.** **The clerk is the detail that makes it land.** **A class that hears only the ten minutes files this as a technique for saving time** — **and a class that hears the clerk understands that the cost of naming a mistake is usually paid by somebody who did not make it.**'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**1** ⚠ _Your question assumes something that is not the case._ → **the mistake named, and the next ten minutes are about it.**   **2** **Stress on _if_** → **a challenge, and the questioner defends the question.**   **3** ⚠ **Only one half of the reframe given** → **a dodge, and it will be read as one.**   **4** **A reframe used on a fair question** → **evasion, and the room is right to hear it.**   **5** ⚠ **A reframe used on a reason question** → **the premise was true and the answer was a reason.**'));
