out.push(SAMPLE(['Mrs Khoury said the note that goes in before the candidate is for choosing which sentence comes back at you, because a panel reads three hundred words and opens with one of them, and a candidate who has not chosen has let the panel choose. Mr Attar said so you write the sentence you want quoted, and you write the sentence you can defend for ten minutes and make it the most quotable thing on the page: short, specific, with its limit inside it, because the panel is not looking for your best sentence but for the one that gives it a question. Mrs Tarabishi said hers was the denominator and they took it: nine findings at four degrees of certainty, each degree stated in the sentence that carries the finding, twenty-one words, and the chair read it out and asked her to take the four degrees in turn, which is forty minutes she had prepared and the only forty minutes she had prepared. Dr Qabbani said that is luck, and it was not: it is the only sentence in the note with a number in it twice, and a panel reading three hundred words of prose will open on the one place where something is countable. Mrs Oueidat asked about the sentences you do not want quoted, and you take them out, and the test is one question: could this be read aloud against me? Mr Shihabi said three hundred words is short for two pages, and it is two pages because of what is on them and not how much: four countable facts, a scope sentence and nothing about the writer, and the white space is doing work. Mr Tayara asked whether nothing about the writer at all, and nothing, because a panel told how hard a document was to write has been given a reason to be kind, and kindness cannot be answered. Mrs Rizk asked about what you cannot do, and the scope sentence goes in and is the second most quoted line in this class\'s notes, because a limitation you introduce is a different object from a limitation the panel finds. Mrs Ajouz said so you hand them the hard question, and you hand them the hard question you have an answer to, which is not the hardest one: seven of ten notes had a scope sentence, all seven were asked about it, and all seven had forty seconds ready. And Mrs Tarabishi said she reads it aloud at the speed a panel reads, which is faster than you think: she read hers in ninety seconds and found two sentences she could not finish in one breath, and the middle is where her limit was.']));
out.push(A('⚠ **Two lines to carry out of this cycle.** **Mrs Tarabishi\'s: the middle is where my limit was** — **which is what reading aloud finds and nothing else does.** **And Mrs Khoury\'s: you hand them the hard question you have an answer to, which is not the same as handing them the hardest one.** **Say the second one twice.** **A room that has understood the first half and not the second will put its worst problem in the note.**'));
